Z||000000||DaveL||12-04-2001||03:31 PM||||I recently bought a used 87 2.5 VIN R motor to replace a dead one, and while it's on the stand, I've been cleaning it up some. Want to take off the DIS brick so I can get familiar with it while it's "easy". The bolts are extremely tight. How hard should it be to get them out? My 10mm socket broke before they did. I surely don't want to break them. If I did, how would I get them out?<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||84Bill||12-04-2001||03:41 PM||||Ghads man! they should not be that tight but it's the only way. Just be glad it's not in the car right now.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Pheagey||12-04-2001||06:14 PM||||Yea, they should be snug enof to stay on but not that bad...<BR> anyways...<P>------------------<BR>'88 coupe, 5 speed, custome paint job<BR>"I! have a plan!"<BR>Have you seen my website yet?<BR>Http://www.LithiumEffects/pheageysfeiro<BR>Killed: '97 civic CS auto;<BR>Killed by: '94 Civic Ex, '96 Camery LX<BR>Vandalised: 2 time (in 2 days).||||reg||1
Z||000003||WKDFIRO||12-04-2001||11:22 PM||||Not sure why they would be so tight but at least one of them will have teflon threading, if memory serves me, its the one on the right that actually goes through an oil galley. MAKE SURE TO REAPPLY THE TEFLON! I forgot and its a true mess.<P>One possibility is that all three of them have teflon and it is sticking, but breaking a socket? Whoa.||||reg||6
Z||000004||theogre||12-04-2001||11:33 PM||||Why yours are so tight I do not know... that ain't normal. Very badly rusted or a cheap socket?<P>I'm pretty sure both top bolts get teflon pipe tape on them.<P>there are a whole bunch of notes in my cave.... including how to work on the brick w/o removing the mounting plate.<P>Keep in mind... those bolts are pretty long... you won't extract them with an "easy out" if they break. It will be strictly a drill and tap job.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000005||erikred||12-05-2001||11:19 AM||||The part of the bolt shaft that passes through the brick is probably corroded. Soak them overnight with a high quality penetrating oil. Apply it several times so it good and soaked. Then while applying constant pressure with a wrench or ratchet tap the end of the wrench or ratchet with a hammer in the same direction you are pulling. This tends to jar the bolt loose while the force you apply to the wrench spins the bolt loose. I have found this to be the best method of getting out frozen bolts without breaking off the head.<P>Erik- ||||reg||12
